Calli builds a machine to force the cosmos into predictable, mathematical harmony. She tames the stars into bronze gear ratios.
Mithridates VI uses that machine to try and eliminate chance, setting a synchronized clock for an empire-wide strike.
Murphy steps in anyway. Calli suffers a quiet, tragic accident; the secret dispersal scatters the builders; and one of the pristine bronze masterworks ends up at the bottom of the Aegean Sea inside a sunken Roman freighter.
And riding right down the middle of that chaotic, unpredictable world is Curt—a man who doesn't expect the gods to make sense, doesn't expect the weather to hold, and names his mule Ox because he knows better than to argue with reality.
